information on to law enforcement, biometric
surveillance, facial recognition software.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:13.070
We're gonna get into some of these specifics
and where they're being used. But what is

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:19.820
the position of the ACL? Uhhh, generally speaking
about law enforcement's ability to use these

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:25.570
different technologies when a warrant does
not exist for the specific surveillance of

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.220
an individual.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:34.070
So just as a general matter, um, the fourth
amendment provides for our right to privacy

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:39.990
and says essentially that unless you're involved
in criminal activity and the government can

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:46.930
show evidence of that which is called probable
cause to a judge and get a warrant, they shouldn't

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:52.690
be able to invade your privacy. Now, as we
kind of discussed before there an active debate

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:58.560
about what privacy means in the 21st century.
So just as an example, a recent Supreme court

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.560
case, um, that was decided in the summer of
2018 last summer called carpenter versus United

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:09.360
States is the most important privacy ruling.
We've seen that the Supreme court in a long

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:18.709
time and that case is particularly important
because it, um, disagreed with one enforcement's

00:04:18.709 --> 00:04:24.810
interpretation of the fourth amendment, which
essentially was that when you go out in public,

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:30.680
you have no right to privacy. So the court
held in a very important ruling in the carpenter

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:37.030
ruling in a decision written by chief justice
Roberts that in fact technology has substantially

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:40.480
changed the balance of power between the government
and the people.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.430
Now, you know, in a free society, in a democracy,
the government ought to know almost nothing

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:50.340
about the general population unless we've
affirmatively disclosed information to the

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.690
government to receive benefits or something
like that. And we should know almost everything

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:59.480
about what the government is doing, new surveillance
technologies. And unfortunately the post nine

00:04:59.480 --> 00:05:05.640
11 hysteria around terrorism has really flipped
that balance. So now the government, and this

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:10.200
is true of even local and state police officers
all the way up to the level of the federal

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:16.160
government at the FBI and the da, et cetera,
they can know almost everything about us often

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:20.520
without a warrant. And we know very little
about what they're doing because their operations

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:25.110
are shrouded in secrecy. So the carpenter
ruling is really important because it sticks

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:31.690
a very clear, uh, claim rather, uh, draws
a line in the sand for 21st century forms

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:38.230
of service surveillance by essentially saying
it is vastly different for one enforcement

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:41.300
to use technology like cell phone tracking.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.750
Um, you know, technologies like cell cell
site location information, information that

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:52.640
is maintained on hundreds of millions of people
across the United States that is qualitatively

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.311
and quantitatively different from law enforcement
following one person around to see where they're

00:05:58.311 --> 00:06:03.950
going. And this is an important concept that
we call friction, right? Um, there are only

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:08.230
so many police officers in the U S I think
there are too many. There are about a million.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:12.670
Um, but there are over 300 million people
