00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:17.950
physically impossible for law enforcement
to follow each one of us around. Um, noting

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:23.430
our every movement, every day of our lives.
Cell phone technology makes it possible not

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:29.120
only for law enforcement to see where we are
in the present moment and on a mass scale,

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:33.530
but also to do something that was previously
impossible, which is to find out where we

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:39.680
were three weeks ago, three months ago, six
months ago before we were even under a criminal

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.460
investigation or suspected of a crime.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:47.320
And so chief justice Roberts says that because
the technology facilitates, um, a new form

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:52.750
of mass tracking that is not possible without
the technology, the government is required

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:57.450
to get a warrant, even if they're merely tracking
our public movements, even if they're not

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.860
tracking our private movements in our home,
even if they're just tracking where we go

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:07.470
on public streets. Because previous to the
existence of this technology, um, that type

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:14.290
of surveillance was actually not even feasible.
Um, so yes, master valence is distinct from

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:19.419
individualized, targeted surveillance that
law enforcement is engaged in because they

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:25.240
believe that someone is actually engaged in
a crime. New technologies, license plate readers,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.990
face surveillance technology, certainly cell
phone tracking, give the government the power

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:35.210
to very cheaply conduct surveillance of hundreds
of millions of people's movements and activities.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:40.580
And again, not just in the present moment,
but actually reversing back in time to see

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:44.449
what we've been doing, uh, days, weeks, or
months before.

